WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.104 --> 00:00:11.789
Aisha Coleman: Hi Denise, thanks for making the time. How's the week going over there?

2
00:00:13.283 --> 00:00:19.280
Denise Morales: Busy. We're down two people on the member care floor, so everyone's picking up extra shifts.

3
00:00:20.352 --> 00:00:25.510
Aisha Coleman: That's a lot. Is that temporary coverage or are you hiring for those seats?

4
00:00:26.702 --> 00:00:34.903
Denise Morales: Hiring, but you know how it goes. Interviews take forever in our HR process. Should be filled by November, hopefully.

5
00:00:36.121 --> 00:00:47.952
Aisha Coleman: Got it. I pulled the numbers before this call so we could walk through them together. Last quarter Harbor had about 61,000 inbound calls across all campaigns.

6
00:00:47.952 --> 00:00:50.580
Aisha Coleman: Does that match what you're seeing?

7
00:00:51.587 --> 00:00:59.166
Denise Morales: Roughly. I think we were closer to 63,000 if you count the overflow line for the loan team.

8
00:01:00.365 --> 00:01:11.833
Aisha Coleman: Okay, I'll check whether that number is pulling into the same campaign group. And the Google Ads side, you're still sending conversions back for the Smart Bidding campaigns?

9
00:01:13.322 --> 00:01:17.830
Denise Morales: Yes, that's still running the way it was. We haven't touched it.

10
00:01:19.047 --> 00:01:25.519
Aisha Coleman: Good. How about Salesforce? Any changes on the Financial Services Cloud side since we talked in June?

11
00:01:26.464 --> 00:01:31.651
Denise Morales: No, same setup. Call records land on the member record like before.

12
00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:39.708
Aisha Coleman: Okay. Let me switch to the quality management piece, since that's what we set aside most of the hour for.

13
00:01:40.758 --> 00:01:45.613
Denise Morales: Yes, please. That's the part I actually wanted to talk through.

14
00:01:46.871 --> 00:01:49.098
Aisha Coleman: Tell me what's been coming up.

15
00:01:49.960 --> 00:02:02.280
Denise Morales: So our QA team has a rubric they've used for years, before we ever had software scoring calls. It has specific things they listen for on each call type.

16
00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:13.750
Denise Morales: A HELOC inquiry has different checkpoints than a fraud report or a card dispute. Right now the scorecard in the platform doesn't map to any of that.

17
00:02:15.024 --> 00:02:18.200
Aisha Coleman: When you say specific checkpoints, what kinds of things?

18
00:02:18.981 --> 00:02:30.314
Denise Morales: Exact disclosures. Reg Z language on lending calls, the fraud liability script, the identity verification steps. Word for word matters to us, not just the gist of it.

19
00:02:30.314 --> 00:02:34.361
Denise Morales: If an agent paraphrases, that's a finding in our rubric.

20
00:02:35.721 --> 00:02:39.476
Aisha Coleman: And the scoring today doesn't let you build that granularity in?

21
00:02:40.403 --> 00:02:56.149
Denise Morales: Not at the level we need. We'd want to define the criteria ourselves, per call type, and weight them. The identity verification piece should carry more weight on a fraud call than it does on a general servicing question.

22
00:02:56.149 --> 00:03:08.664
Denise Morales: Right now everything's scored the same way across the board and our compliance reviews come back asking why the scores don't line up with what the QA team would have given.

23
00:03:09.578 --> 00:03:17.525
Aisha Coleman: So the ask is essentially: let Harbor build scorecards that mirror your existing rubric, with call-type specific questions and weighting.

24
00:03:18.571 --> 00:03:26.891
Denise Morales: That's it exactly. If the platform's scoring lined up with the rubric my team already uses, we wouldn't have to run two parallel QA processes.

25
00:03:26.891 --> 00:03:31.882
Denise Morales: And our compliance reviews would get simpler because there'd be one number instead of two.

26
00:03:32.428 --> 00:03:35.758
Aisha Coleman: How often does compliance review the scorecards today?

27
00:03:36.225 --> 00:03:47.389
Denise Morales: Every time we change one. Our internal audit group signs off on any scoring change before it goes live, so we've been pretty conservative about what we adjust.

28
00:03:48.479 --> 00:03:59.344
Aisha Coleman: Understood. I'm going to write this up for our product team with the examples you gave, Reg Z, fraud liability, identity verification, and the weighting piece.

29
00:03:59.344 --> 00:04:05.194
Aisha Coleman: Can I use Harbor's name in that write-up, or do you want it anonymized?

30
00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:09.923
Denise Morales: You can use our name. No issue there.

31
00:04:10.684 --> 00:04:19.566
Aisha Coleman: Great. I can't promise a timeline from here, but I'll get it in front of the right people and let you know what I hear back.

32
00:04:19.566 --> 00:04:21.616
Aisha Coleman: Anything else on the QA side?

33
00:04:22.059 --> 00:04:27.830
Denise Morales: No, that's the one. Everything else on that end is working the way we expect.

34
00:04:28.371 --> 00:04:42.228
Aisha Coleman: Okay. Last thing from my side, your renewal comes up in February 2027. We don't need to do anything now, but I'll start the internal prep on that after the first of the year so it's not a scramble.

35
00:04:43.308 --> 00:04:52.361
Denise Morales: That works. I'll be on leave the first two weeks of January, so maybe aim for the back half of the month.

36
00:04:52.679 --> 00:05:01.698
Aisha Coleman: Noted, back half of January. Do you want me to send a recap of today with the numbers and the scorecard request?

37
00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:07.240
Denise Morales: Yes, send it to me and I'll forward it to my director.

38
00:05:08.254 --> 00:05:11.647
Aisha Coleman: Will do, probably this afternoon. Thanks for the time, Denise.

39
00:05:12.265 --> 00:05:13.664
Denise Morales: Thanks, Aisha. Talk soon.
